<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  14.2 Limits and Continuity in Higher Dimensions
-->
<section xml:id="chapter-limits-continuity" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Limits and Continuity in Higher Dimensions</title>

<objectives>
<introduction>
<p>
After completing this section, you should be able to:
</p>
</introduction>

<ul>
<li>
<p>
Explain what it means for
<m>\lim_{(x,y)\to(a,b)} f(x,y)</m> to exist, and why the value must be
the same along every path of approach.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Evaluate limits of functions of two variables using direct substitution,
algebraic simplification, and the limit laws.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Show that a limit does not exist by finding two paths along which the
function approaches different values.
</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>
Decide whether a function of two variables is continuous at a point, and
identify the points at which a piecewise-defined function fails to be
continuous.
</p>
</li>
</ul>
</objectives>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-lim-two-variable">
<title>Limits of Two-Variable Functions</title>

<p>
For <m>y = f(x)</m>, we could ask what value the function approaches when
<m>x</m> approaches <m>a \in \R</m>, and there were two possible
directions (from the left and from the right) to approach <m>x = a</m>,
which we denoted by <m>\lim_{x\to a^-} f(x)</m> and
<m>\lim_{x\to a^+} f(x)</m>. If
<m>\lim_{x\to a^-} f(x) = \lim_{x\to a^+} f(x) = L \in \R</m>, then
<m>\lim_{x\to a} f(x) = L</m>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-lim-one-variable">
<caption>
For <m>y = f(x)</m> there are only two ways to approach <m>x = a</m>:
from the left and from the right. When both one-sided limits agree, their
common value <m>L</m> is <m>\lim_{x\to a} f(x)</m>.
</caption>
<image width="66%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-lim-one-variable">
<diagram dimensions="(420,280)" margins="12">
<definition>f(x) = 1.8 + 0.42*(x-2.2) - 0.18*(x-2.2)^2</definition>
<definition>curve(t) = (t, f(t))</definition>
<!-- the two one-sided approaches, each parametrised to land on x = a at t = 1 -->
<definition>left(t) = (2.2 - 1.05*(1-t), f(2.2 - 1.05*(1-t)))</definition>
<definition>right(t) = (2.2 + 1.15*(1-t), f(2.2 + 1.15*(1-t)))</definition>
<!-- the same two approaches seen on the x-axis, where x itself moves toward a -->
<definition>xleft(t) = (1.15 + 0.93*t, 0)</definition>
<definition>xright(t) = (3.35 - 1.03*t, 0)</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-0.6,-0.55,4.7,3.05)">
<axes at="l1-axes" xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="no" arrows="1"/>
<parametric-curve at="l1-curve" function="curve" domain="(0.3,4.35)"
                  stroke="#555555" thickness="2"/>
<line at="l1-vert" endpoints="((2.2,0),(2.2,1.8))" stroke="#888888"
      dash="3 3" thickness="1"/>
<line at="l1-horiz" endpoints="((0,1.8),(2.2,1.8))" stroke="#888888"
      dash="3 3" thickness="1"/>
<parametric-curve at="l1-left" function="left" domain="(0,1)"
                  stroke="#0072B2" thickness="4" arrows="1"
                  arrow-location="0.65"/>
<parametric-curve at="l1-right" function="right" domain="(0,1)"
                  stroke="#D55E00" thickness="4" arrows="1"
                  arrow-location="0.65"/>
<parametric-curve at="l1-xleft" function="xleft" domain="(0,1)"
                  stroke="#0072B2" thickness="4" arrows="1"
                  arrow-location="0.95"/>
<parametric-curve at="l1-xright" function="xright" domain="(0,1)"
                  stroke="#D55E00" thickness="4" arrows="1"
                  arrow-location="0.95"/>
<point at="l1-P" p="(2.2,1.8)" fill="black"/>
<label at="l1-left-lab" anchor="(1.18,1.0)" alignment="se" color="#0072B2">
<m>f(x) \to L</m>
</label>
<label at="l1-right-lab" anchor="(3.2,2.16)" alignment="ne" color="#D55E00">
<m>f(x) \to L</m>
</label>
<label at="l1-xleft-lab" anchor="(1.45,-0.12)" alignment="s" color="#0072B2">
<m>x \to a^-</m>
</label>
<label at="l1-xright-lab" anchor="(3.05,-0.12)" alignment="s" color="#D55E00">
<m>x \to a^+</m>
</label>
<label at="l1-a" anchor="(2.2,-0.06)" alignment="s"><m>a</m></label>
<label at="l1-L" anchor="(-0.06,1.8)" alignment="w"><m>L</m></label>
<label at="l1-f" anchor="(4.35,1.55)" alignment="sw" color="#555555">
<m>y=f(x)</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="The graph of a function y equals f of x in the plane. A point on the graph above x equals a is marked, and dashed lines run from it to a on the x axis and to L on the y axis. On the x axis two thick arrows run towards a, a blue one from the left labelled x tending to a from below and an orange one from the right labelled x tending to a from above. The matching pieces of the graph are drawn thick in the same colours and are labelled f of x tending to L, so the point is approached from only two directions.">
  <annotation ref="l1-xleft" text="The approach from the left along the x axis, x tending to a from below."/>
  <annotation ref="l1-xright" text="The approach from the right along the x axis, x tending to a from above."/>
  <annotation ref="l1-left" text="The piece of the graph over the approach from the left. Along it the height of the graph tends to L."/>
  <annotation ref="l1-right" text="The piece of the graph over the approach from the right. Along it too the height tends to L."/>
  <annotation ref="l1-P" text="The point a comma L on the graph. Because the two one sided limits agree, the limit of f as x tends to a is L."/>
  <annotation ref="l1-axes" text="On the line there are only these two directions of approach, which is what changes in two variables."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
In two variables the situation is richer, because there are infinitely
many directions, and indeed infinitely many curved paths, along which a
point can approach <m>(a,b)</m>. This section makes that precise and
develops the tools for computing such limits, and for showing that a
limit fails to exist.
</p>

<p>
Consider the function <m>z = f(x,y)</m> that we studied in the previous
section. Below are some key points regarding the limit of such a
function.
</p>

<p>
<alert>I.</alert> There are infinitely many ways of approaching a point
<m>(a,b)</m>. A few such paths are drawn in green in
<xref ref="fig-lim-paths"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-lim-paths">
<caption>
A few of the infinitely many paths along which a point can approach
<m>(a,b)</m>. The paths may be straight lines or curves, and they may
arrive from any direction.
</caption>
<image width="66%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-lim-paths">
<diagram dimensions="(400,300)" margins="12">
<!-- every path is parametrised so that t = 1 lands exactly on (a,b) -->
<definition>p1(t) = (1.55 + 0.38*(1-t)*sin(4*(1-t)), 1.0 + 1.65*(1-t))</definition>
<definition>p2(t) = (1.55 - 1.45*(1-t), 1.0 + 0.85*(1-t)^2)</definition>
<definition>p3(t) = (1.55 + 1.85*(1-t), 1.0 + 0.80*(1-t)^2)</definition>
<definition>p4(t) = (1.55 + 1.55*(1-t) - 0.30*(1-t)^2, 1.0 - 0.95*(1-t))</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-0.55,-0.5,4.2,3.2)">
<axes at="lp-axes" xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="no" arrows="1"/>
<parametric-curve at="lp-c1" function="p1" domain="(0,1)"
                  stroke="#009E73" thickness="3" arrows="1" arrow-location="0.72"/>
<parametric-curve at="lp-c2" function="p2" domain="(0,1)"
                  stroke="#009E73" thickness="3" arrows="1" arrow-location="0.72"/>
<parametric-curve at="lp-c3" function="p3" domain="(0,1)"
                  stroke="#009E73" thickness="3" arrows="1" arrow-location="0.72"/>
<parametric-curve at="lp-c4" function="p4" domain="(0,1)"
                  stroke="#009E73" thickness="3" arrows="1" arrow-location="0.72"/>
<line at="lp-hx" endpoints="((0,1.0),(1.55,1.0))" stroke="#D55E00"
      dash="3 3" thickness="1.5"/>
<line at="lp-hy" endpoints="((1.55,0),(1.55,1.0))" stroke="#D55E00"
      dash="3 3" thickness="1.5"/>
<point at="lp-P" p="(1.55,1.0)" fill="#D55E00"/>
<label at="lp-P-lab" anchor="(1.49,1.07)" alignment="nw" color="#D55E00">
<m>(a,b)</m>
</label>
<label at="lp-a" anchor="(1.55,-0.06)" alignment="s" color="#D55E00">
<m>a</m>
</label>
<label at="lp-b" anchor="(-0.06,1.0)" alignment="w" color="#D55E00">
<m>b</m>
</label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="A coordinate plane with the point a comma b marked in orange, located by dashed lines dropped to the two axes. Four green curves of different shapes come in towards that point from different directions, each ending in an arrowhead at the point, showing that a point may approach a comma b along infinitely many different paths.">
  <annotation ref="lp-P" text="The point a comma b that is being approached."/>
  <annotation ref="lp-c1" text="One path approaching the point from above, curving as it comes in, with an arrowhead at the point."/>
  <annotation ref="lp-c2" text="A second path approaching from the upper left, with an arrowhead at the point."/>
  <annotation ref="lp-c3" text="A third path approaching from the upper right along a curve, with an arrowhead at the point."/>
  <annotation ref="lp-c4" text="A fourth path approaching from below. For the limit to exist, the function must approach the same value along every one of these paths."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
<alert>II.</alert> The limit
<m>\lim_{(x,y)\to(a,b)} f(x,y)</m> exists if, regardless of the path
taken to approach the point <m>(a,b)</m>, the value of the function
<m>f(x,y)</m> approaches the same value.
</p>

<p>
<alert>III.</alert> To show that
<m>\lim_{(x,y)\to(a,b)} f(x,y)</m> does not exist, it suffices to show
that the value of the function approaches two different values for two
different paths taken to move towards <m>(a,b)</m>.
</p>

<example xml:id="example-lim-dne">
<title>A Limit That Does Not Exist</title>
<statement>
<p>
Consider the function <m>f(x,y) = \dfrac{xy}{x^2+y^2}</m>. Does
<m>\lim_{(x,y)\to(0,0)} f(x,y)</m> exist?
</p>
</statement>

<solution>
<p>
We begin by approaching the point <m>(0,0)</m> along the line
<m>y = 0</m>. Note that along the line <m>y = 0</m>, we have
<md>
f(x,0) = \frac{0}{x^2+0^2} = 0,
</md>
and hence
<md>
f(x,0) \to 0 \quad \text{as } x \to 0.
</md>
Similarly, along <m>x = 0</m>,
<md>
f(0,y) \to 0 \quad \text{as } y \to 0.
</md>
</p>
<p>
Let us next see what happens when approaching the point <m>(0,0)</m>
along the line <m>y = x</m>. We have
<md>
f(x,x) = \frac{x^2}{x^2+x^2} = \frac{1}{2},
\qquad \text{(note that } x \neq 0 \text{)}
</md>
and hence
<md>
f(x,x) \to \frac{1}{2} \quad \text{as } x \to 0.
</md>
</p>
<p>
As was shown above and is depicted in
<xref ref="fig-lim-three-paths"/>, when approaching along different
paths the function's value approaches different values: zero along
<m>y = 0</m> and <m>x = 0</m>, and <m>\frac12</m> along <m>y = x</m>.
Therefore we can conclude that the limit does not exist.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-lim-three-paths">
<caption>
Three paths of approach to the origin for
<m>f(x,y) = \frac{xy}{x^2+y^2}</m>. The function is constantly <m>0</m>
along the two axes and constantly <m>\frac12</m> along the line
<m>y=x</m>, so no single value is approached and the limit does not
exist.
</caption>
<image width="62%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-lim-three-paths">
<diagram dimensions="(360,300)" margins="12">
<definition>diag(t) = (t, t)</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-2.4,-2.2,2.6,2.4)">
<axes at="tp-axes" xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="no" arrows="1"/>
<parametric-curve at="tp-diag" function="diag" domain="(-1.95,1.95)"
                  stroke="#D55E00" thickness="2.5"/>
<line at="tp-xaxis" endpoints="((-2.1,0),(2.1,0))" stroke="#D55E00"
      thickness="2.5"/>
<line at="tp-yaxis" endpoints="((0,-1.9),(0,1.9))" stroke="#D55E00"
      thickness="2.5"/>
<point at="tp-O" p="(0,0)" fill="#D55E00"/>
<label at="tp-diag-lab" anchor="(1.72,1.86)" alignment="ne" color="#D55E00">
<m>y=x</m>
</label>
<label at="tp-f-diag" anchor="(1.05,0.62)" alignment="se" color="#D55E00">
<m>f=\tfrac12</m>
</label>
<label at="tp-f-x" anchor="(1.85,-0.12)" alignment="se" color="#D55E00">
<m>f=0</m>
</label>
<label at="tp-f-y" anchor="(-0.14,1.62)" alignment="nw" color="#D55E00">
<m>f=0</m>
</label>
<label at="tp-x" anchor="(2.42,-0.04)" alignment="se"><m>x</m></label>
<label at="tp-y" anchor="(-0.04,2.25)" alignment="nw"><m>y</m></label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="The x and y axes and the diagonal line y equals x, all drawn in orange and all passing through the origin. The two axes are labelled f equals zero and the diagonal is labelled f equals one half, showing that the function takes different constant values along different lines through the origin.">
  <annotation ref="tp-xaxis" text="The path along the x axis, where y equals zero. Along it the function is constantly zero."/>
  <annotation ref="tp-yaxis" text="The path along the y axis, where x equals zero. Along it the function is also constantly zero."/>
  <annotation ref="tp-diag" text="The path along the line y equals x. Along it the function is constantly one half, a different value, so the limit at the origin does not exist."/>
  <annotation ref="tp-O" text="The origin, the point being approached."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
<xref ref="fig-lim-paths-interactive"/> puts the same three paths on the
surface itself. Click a path on the floor of the figure &#8212; or the
matching button beneath it &#8212; and the piece of the surface above that
path is drawn in. The surface is built so that its height depends only on
the <em>direction</em> of approach, which is why each lifted path is a
level line: whichever of the three you follow in, the height you arrive at
is the one your path started with.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-lim-paths-interactive">
<caption>
The three paths of <xref ref="fig-lim-three-paths"/>, lifted onto the
surface <m>z = \dfrac{xy}{x^2+y^2}</m>. Choosing <m>y=0</m> or <m>x=0</m>
lifts to a path at height <m>0</m>, while <m>y=x</m> lifts to one at
height <m>\frac12</m>, so the two approaches deliver two different
numbers and the limit cannot exist.
</caption>
<!-- aspect 6:5 rather than 3:2: the equation card and the value readout sit
     in a bar above the picture (and the caption in a strip below it) instead
     of overlaying it, so the frame needs the extra height to keep the
     surface itself the same size. -->
<interactive xml:id="interactive-lim-paths"
             iframe="limit-paths-manim.html" width="100%" aspect="6:5">
<instructions>
<p>
The floor of the picture is the <m>xy</m>-plane and the surface above it
is <m>z = f(x,y) = \frac{xy}{x^2+y^2}</m>. The three coloured lines on the
floor are the paths <m>y=0</m>, <m>x=0</m> and <m>y=x</m>; click one, or
use the buttons, to draw the part of the surface lying over it. The value
that path approaches is written beside it and collected in the panel
above the picture. <q>Approach <m>(0,0)</m></q> slides a point in along every
chosen path at once, <q>Top view</q> looks straight down at the three
lines, and dragging turns the picture.
</p>
</instructions>
</interactive>
</figure>

<figure xml:id="fig-lim-asymptote-x-axis">
<caption>
The path <m>y=0</m> from <xref ref="fig-lim-paths-interactive"/>,
lifted onto the surface. Since <m>f(x,0)=0</m> for <m>x\ne0</m>,
the lifted path approaches height <m>0</m>. The domain path is
shown on a lower reference plane to separate it from its surface trace;
the open circle marks a limiting position where the function is undefined.
</caption>
<image width="90%">
<shortdescription>A rotatable surface with the path y=0 highlighted and its trace at height zero.</shortdescription>
<description><p>
The surface is z equals x y divided by x squared plus y squared, drawn
over a punctured disk with a small hole at the origin. The green trace lies at height zero on both sides of the missing point over the origin.
A matching path is drawn on a reference plane below the surface, with
vertical guide lines connecting it to the trace. Arrows point inward
from both sides. Dragging rotates the diagram.
</p></description>
<asymptote label="asymptote-lim-x-axis"><![CDATA[
import graph3;
viewportmargin=(16,16);
// Retain interactive WebGL for HTML and vector graphics for print.
if(settings.outformat != "html") settings.render=0;
size(12cm,10cm);
triple eye=(4,-7,4);
currentprojection=orthographic(camera=eye,up=Z,target=(0,0,-0.25));
currentlight=light(gray(0.65),specularfactor=0.15,(4,-3,6),(-4,-1,3));

// xy/(x^2+y^2) = (1/2) sin(2 theta), independent of radius.
// Exclude r=0: the function has no value at the origin.
real inner=0.06, outer=1.7, floorZ=-1.05;
triple surfacePoint(pair p) {
  return (p.x*cos(p.y),p.x*sin(p.y),0.5*sin(2*p.y));
}
surface s=surface(surfacePoint,(inner,0),(outer,2*pi),8,96);
draw(s,surfacepen=gray(0.82)+opacity(0.25),
     meshpen=gray(0.65)+opacity(0.15)+linewidth(0.15pt));

// This lower grid is a diagrammatic copy of the domain, not z=0.
pen grid=gray(0.8)+linewidth(0.3pt);
for(int i=-3;i<=3;++i) {
  real u=0.55*i;
  draw((-1.8,u,floorZ)--(1.8,u,floorZ),grid);
  draw((u,-1.8,floorZ)--(u,1.8,floorZ),grid);
}
pen axisPen=gray(0.35)+linewidth(0.6pt);
draw(Label("$x$",1),(-1.9,0,floorZ)--(2.1,0,floorZ),axisPen,Arrow3);
draw(Label("$y$",1),(0,-1.9,floorZ)--(0,2.1,floorZ),axisPen,Arrow3);
draw(Label("$z$",1),(0,0,-0.65)--(0,0,1.0),axisPen+dashed,Arrow3);
label("$0$",(0,0,0),W,gray(0.35));
label("$\frac12$",(0,0,0.5),W,gray(0.35));

real theta=0, level=0;
triple direction=(cos(theta),sin(theta),0);
pen accent=rgb("83C167");
pen tracePen=accent+linewidth(2.6pt);
// Draw both halves separately so the highlighted trace never fills the hole.
for(int side=-1;side<=1;side+=2) {
  triple far=side*outer*direction;
  triple near=side*inner*direction;
  draw(far+floorZ*Z--near+floorZ*Z,accent+linewidth(1.8pt),Arrow3(size=5));
  draw(far+level*Z--near+level*Z,tracePen,Arrow3(size=5));
  for(int j=1;j<=5;++j) {
    triple v=side*(outer*j/5.5)*direction;
    draw(v+floorZ*Z--v+level*Z,accent+opacity(0.5)+dashed+linewidth(0.5pt));
  }
}
// A ring rather than a filled dot: this is a limit, not a defined value.
draw(circle((0,0,level),0.045,eye),accent+linewidth(1.4pt));
dot((0,0,floorZ),gray(0.35)+linewidth(3pt));
label("$(0,0)$",(0,0,floorZ),SW,gray(0.35));
label("$y=0$",1.55*direction+floorZ*Z+0.3*(sin(theta),-cos(theta),0),S,accent);
label("$f=0$",-1.1*direction+(level+0.13)*Z,N,accent);
label("$z=\dfrac{xy}{x^2+y^2}$",(-2.1,1.5,1.0),E,gray(0.3));
currentpicture.addBox((-2.2,-2.2,-1.2),(2.2,2.2,1.35));
]]></asymptote>
</image>
</figure>

<figure xml:id="fig-lim-asymptote-y-axis">
<caption>
The path <m>x=0</m> from <xref ref="fig-lim-paths-interactive"/>,
lifted onto the surface. Since <m>f(0,y)=0</m> for <m>y\ne0</m>,
the lifted path approaches height <m>0</m>. The domain path is
shown on a lower reference plane to separate it from its surface trace;
the open circle marks a limiting position where the function is undefined.
</caption>
<image width="90%">
<shortdescription>A rotatable surface with the path x=0 highlighted and its trace at height zero.</shortdescription>
<description><p>
The surface is z equals x y divided by x squared plus y squared, drawn
over a punctured disk with a small hole at the origin. The blue trace lies at height zero on both sides of the missing point over the origin.
A matching path is drawn on a reference plane below the surface, with
vertical guide lines connecting it to the trace. Arrows point inward
from both sides. Dragging rotates the diagram.
</p></description>
<asymptote label="asymptote-lim-y-axis"><![CDATA[
import graph3;
viewportmargin=(16,16);
// Retain interactive WebGL for HTML and vector graphics for print.
if(settings.outformat != "html") settings.render=0;
size(12cm,10cm);
triple eye=(4,-7,4);
currentprojection=orthographic(camera=eye,up=Z,target=(0,0,-0.25));
currentlight=light(gray(0.65),specularfactor=0.15,(4,-3,6),(-4,-1,3));

// xy/(x^2+y^2) = (1/2) sin(2 theta), independent of radius.
// Exclude r=0: the function has no value at the origin.
real inner=0.06, outer=1.7, floorZ=-1.05;
triple surfacePoint(pair p) {
  return (p.x*cos(p.y),p.x*sin(p.y),0.5*sin(2*p.y));
}
surface s=surface(surfacePoint,(inner,0),(outer,2*pi),8,96);
draw(s,surfacepen=gray(0.82)+opacity(0.25),
     meshpen=gray(0.65)+opacity(0.15)+linewidth(0.15pt));

// This lower grid is a diagrammatic copy of the domain, not z=0.
pen grid=gray(0.8)+linewidth(0.3pt);
for(int i=-3;i<=3;++i) {
  real u=0.55*i;
  draw((-1.8,u,floorZ)--(1.8,u,floorZ),grid);
  draw((u,-1.8,floorZ)--(u,1.8,floorZ),grid);
}
pen axisPen=gray(0.35)+linewidth(0.6pt);
draw(Label("$x$",1),(-1.9,0,floorZ)--(2.1,0,floorZ),axisPen,Arrow3);
draw(Label("$y$",1),(0,-1.9,floorZ)--(0,2.1,floorZ),axisPen,Arrow3);
draw(Label("$z$",1),(0,0,-0.65)--(0,0,1.0),axisPen+dashed,Arrow3);
label("$0$",(0,0,0),W,gray(0.35));
label("$\frac12$",(0,0,0.5),W,gray(0.35));

real theta=pi/2, level=0;
triple direction=(cos(theta),sin(theta),0);
pen accent=rgb("58C4DD");
pen tracePen=accent+linewidth(2.6pt);
// Draw both halves separately so the highlighted trace never fills the hole.
for(int side=-1;side<=1;side+=2) {
  triple far=side*outer*direction;
  triple near=side*inner*direction;
  draw(far+floorZ*Z--near+floorZ*Z,accent+linewidth(1.8pt),Arrow3(size=5));
  draw(far+level*Z--near+level*Z,tracePen,Arrow3(size=5));
  for(int j=1;j<=5;++j) {
    triple v=side*(outer*j/5.5)*direction;
    draw(v+floorZ*Z--v+level*Z,accent+opacity(0.5)+dashed+linewidth(0.5pt));
  }
}
// A ring rather than a filled dot: this is a limit, not a defined value.
draw(circle((0,0,level),0.045,eye),accent+linewidth(1.4pt));
dot((0,0,floorZ),gray(0.35)+linewidth(3pt));
label("$(0,0)$",(0,0,floorZ),SW,gray(0.35));
label("$x=0$",1.55*direction+floorZ*Z+0.3*(sin(theta),-cos(theta),0),S,accent);
label("$f=0$",-1.1*direction+(level+0.13)*Z,N,accent);
label("$z=\dfrac{xy}{x^2+y^2}$",(-2.1,1.5,1.0),E,gray(0.3));
currentpicture.addBox((-2.2,-2.2,-1.2),(2.2,2.2,1.35));
]]></asymptote>
</image>
</figure>

<figure xml:id="fig-lim-asymptote-diagonal">
<caption>
The path <m>y=x</m> from <xref ref="fig-lim-paths-interactive"/>,
lifted onto the surface. Since <m>f(x,x)=\frac12</m> for <m>x\ne0</m>,
the lifted path approaches height <m>\frac12</m>. The domain path is
shown on a lower reference plane to separate it from its surface trace;
the open circle marks a limiting position where the function is undefined.
This differs from the value along either axis, so the two-variable limit
does not exist.
</caption>
<image width="90%">
<shortdescription>A rotatable surface with the path y=x highlighted and its trace at height one half.</shortdescription>
<description><p>
The surface is z equals x y divided by x squared plus y squared, drawn
over a punctured disk with a small hole at the origin. The gold trace lies at height one half on both sides of the missing point over the origin, unlike the height zero traces along the axes.
A matching path is drawn on a reference plane below the surface, with
vertical guide lines connecting it to the trace. Arrows point inward
from both sides. Dragging rotates the diagram.
</p></description>
<asymptote label="asymptote-lim-diagonal"><![CDATA[
import graph3;
viewportmargin=(16,16);
// Retain interactive WebGL for HTML and vector graphics for print.
if(settings.outformat != "html") settings.render=0;
size(12cm,10cm);
triple eye=(4,-7,4);
currentprojection=orthographic(camera=eye,up=Z,target=(0,0,-0.25));
currentlight=light(gray(0.65),specularfactor=0.15,(4,-3,6),(-4,-1,3));

// xy/(x^2+y^2) = (1/2) sin(2 theta), independent of radius.
// Exclude r=0: the function has no value at the origin.
real inner=0.06, outer=1.7, floorZ=-1.05;
triple surfacePoint(pair p) {
  return (p.x*cos(p.y),p.x*sin(p.y),0.5*sin(2*p.y));
}
surface s=surface(surfacePoint,(inner,0),(outer,2*pi),8,96);
draw(s,surfacepen=gray(0.82)+opacity(0.25),
     meshpen=gray(0.65)+opacity(0.15)+linewidth(0.15pt));

// This lower grid is a diagrammatic copy of the domain, not z=0.
pen grid=gray(0.8)+linewidth(0.3pt);
for(int i=-3;i<=3;++i) {
  real u=0.55*i;
  draw((-1.8,u,floorZ)--(1.8,u,floorZ),grid);
  draw((u,-1.8,floorZ)--(u,1.8,floorZ),grid);
}
pen axisPen=gray(0.35)+linewidth(0.6pt);
draw(Label("$x$",1),(-1.9,0,floorZ)--(2.1,0,floorZ),axisPen,Arrow3);
draw(Label("$y$",1),(0,-1.9,floorZ)--(0,2.1,floorZ),axisPen,Arrow3);
draw(Label("$z$",1),(0,0,-0.65)--(0,0,1.0),axisPen+dashed,Arrow3);
label("$0$",(0,0,0),W,gray(0.35));
label("$\frac12$",(0,0,0.5),W,gray(0.35));

real theta=pi/4, level=0.5;
triple direction=(cos(theta),sin(theta),0);
pen accent=rgb("F0AC5F");
pen tracePen=accent+linewidth(2.6pt);
// Draw both halves separately so the highlighted trace never fills the hole.
for(int side=-1;side<=1;side+=2) {
  triple far=side*outer*direction;
  triple near=side*inner*direction;
  draw(far+floorZ*Z--near+floorZ*Z,accent+linewidth(1.8pt),Arrow3(size=5));
  draw(far+level*Z--near+level*Z,tracePen,Arrow3(size=5));
  for(int j=1;j<=5;++j) {
    triple v=side*(outer*j/5.5)*direction;
    draw(v+floorZ*Z--v+level*Z,accent+opacity(0.5)+dashed+linewidth(0.5pt));
  }
}
// A ring rather than a filled dot: this is a limit, not a defined value.
draw(circle((0,0,level),0.045,eye),accent+linewidth(1.4pt));
dot((0,0,floorZ),gray(0.35)+linewidth(3pt));
label("$(0,0)$",(0,0,floorZ),SW,gray(0.35));
label("$y=x$",1.55*direction+floorZ*Z+0.3*(sin(theta),-cos(theta),0),S,accent);
label("$f=\frac12$",-1.1*direction+(level+0.13)*Z,N,accent);
label("$z=\dfrac{xy}{x^2+y^2}$",(-2.1,1.5,1.0),E,gray(0.3));
currentpicture.addBox((-2.2,-2.2,-1.2),(2.2,2.2,1.35));
]]></asymptote>
</image>
</figure>

</solution>
</example>

<p>
<alert>IV.</alert> Some useful properties of limits are stated in
<xref ref="thm-lim-properties"/>.
</p>

<theorem xml:id="thm-lim-properties">
<title>Properties of Limits</title>
<statement>
<p>
The following rules hold if <m>L</m>, <m>M</m>, and <m>k</m> are real
numbers and
<md>
\lim_{(x,y)\to(x_0,y_0)} f(x,y) = L
\qquad \text{and} \qquad
\lim_{(x,y)\to(x_0,y_0)} g(x,y) = M.
</md>
</p>
<p>
<alert>1. Sum Rule:</alert>
<m>\displaystyle \lim_{(x,y)\to(x_0,y_0)} \bigl(f(x,y)+g(x,y)\bigr)
= L + M</m>
</p>
<p>
<alert>2. Difference Rule:</alert>
<m>\displaystyle \lim_{(x,y)\to(x_0,y_0)} \bigl(f(x,y)-g(x,y)\bigr)
= L - M</m>
</p>
<p>
<alert>3. Product Rule:</alert>
<m>\displaystyle \lim_{(x,y)\to(x_0,y_0)} \bigl(f(x,y)\cdot g(x,y)\bigr)
= L \cdot M</m>
</p>
<p>
<alert>4. Constant Multiple Rule:</alert>
<m>\displaystyle \lim_{(x,y)\to(x_0,y_0)} k f(x,y) = kL</m>
for any number <m>k</m>
</p>
<p>
<alert>5. Quotient Rule:</alert>
<m>\displaystyle \lim_{(x,y)\to(x_0,y_0)} \frac{f(x,y)}{g(x,y)}
= \frac{L}{M}</m>, provided <m>M \neq 0</m>
</p>
<p>
<alert>6. Power Rule:</alert> If <m>r</m> and <m>s</m> are integers with
no common factors and <m>s \neq 0</m>, then
<m>\displaystyle \lim_{(x,y)\to(x_0,y_0)} \bigl(f(x,y)\bigr)^{r/s}
= L^{r/s}</m>, provided <m>L^{r/s}</m> is a real number. (If <m>s</m> is
even, we assume that <m>L \gt 0</m>.)
</p>
</statement>
</theorem>

<example xml:id="example-lim-substitution">
<title>Evaluating a Limit by Direct Substitution</title>
<statement>
<p>
Evaluate
<md>
\lim_{(x,y)\to(2,2\pi)} \frac{x}{y} + \cos(xy).
</md>
</p>
</statement>

<solution>
<p>
Note that we can use the direct substitution property to write
<md>
<mrow>\lim_{(x,y)\to(2,2\pi)} \frac{x}{y} \amp = \frac{2}{2\pi} = \frac{1}{\pi},</mrow>
<mrow>\lim_{(x,y)\to(2,2\pi)} \cos(xy) \amp = \cos(4\pi) = 1.</mrow>
</md>
Then we use the sum rule to write
<md>
\lim_{(x,y)\to(2,2\pi)} \frac{x}{y} + \cos(xy) = \frac{1}{\pi} + 1.
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</example>

<example xml:id="example-lim-simplify">
<title>Evaluating a Limit by Simplifying</title>
<statement>
<p>
Evaluate
<md>
\lim_{(x,y)\to(1,1)}
\frac{\frac{x^2}{2} - xy + \frac{y^2}{2}}{x^2 - y^2}.
</md>
</p>
</statement>

<solution>
<p>
We begin by simplifying the fraction. The numerator is
<m>\frac12\left(x^2 - 2xy + y^2\right) = \frac12 (x-y)^2</m> and the
denominator factors as <m>(x-y)(x+y)</m>, so
<md alignment="gather">
<mrow>\lim_{(x,y)\to(1,1)}
\frac{\frac{x^2}{2} - xy + \frac{y^2}{2}}{x^2 - y^2}</mrow>
<mrow>= \lim_{(x,y)\to(1,1)} \frac{1}{2}\,\frac{(x-y)^2}{(x-y)(x+y)}</mrow>
<mrow>= \frac{1}{2} \lim_{(x,y)\to(1,1)} \frac{x-y}{x+y}</mrow>
<mrow>= 0.</mrow>
</md>
The cancellation is legitimate because <m>x \neq y</m> along the
approach, and the final limit is evaluated by direct substitution, giving
<m>\frac{1-1}{1+1} = 0</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</example>

<example xml:id="example-lim-sine-factor">
<title>Factoring and the Limit of <m>\frac{\sin u}{u}</m></title>
<statement>
<p>
Evaluate
<md>
\lim_{(x,y)\to(1,1)} \frac{\sin(x-y)\left(x^3-y^3\right)}{(x-y)^2(x+y)}.
</md>
</p>
</statement>

<solution>
<p>
Direct substitution gives <m>\frac{0}{0}</m>, so we simplify first. The
difference of cubes factors as
<m>x^3 - y^3 = (x-y)\left(x^2+xy+y^2\right)</m>, so one factor of
<m>x-y</m> cancels against the denominator:
<md>
\frac{\sin(x-y)\left(x^3-y^3\right)}{(x-y)^2(x+y)}
= \frac{\sin(x-y)}{x-y}\cdot\frac{x^2+xy+y^2}{x+y}.
</md>
The cancellation is legitimate because <m>x \neq y</m> along the
approach (the function is not defined on the line <m>y = x</m>).
</p>
<p>
For the first factor, put <m>u = x - y</m>. As
<m>(x,y)\to(1,1)</m> we have <m>u \to 0</m> with <m>u \neq 0</m>, so the
one-variable limit <m>\lim_{u\to 0}\frac{\sin u}{u} = 1</m> gives
<md>
\lim_{(x,y)\to(1,1)} \frac{\sin(x-y)}{x-y} = \lim_{u\to 0}\frac{\sin u}{u} = 1.
</md>
The second factor is evaluated by direct substitution, since its
denominator approaches <m>1 + 1 = 2 \neq 0</m>:
<md>
\lim_{(x,y)\to(1,1)} \frac{x^2+xy+y^2}{x+y} = \frac{1+1+1}{1+1} = \frac{3}{2}.
</md>
By the product rule of <xref ref="thm-lim-properties"/>, the requested limit is
<md>
1\cdot\frac{3}{2} = \frac{3}{2}.
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</example>

<example xml:id="example-lim-curved-path">
<title>Straight Lines Are Not Enough</title>
<statement>
<p>
Evaluate the following limit, or show that it does not exist.
<md>
\lim_{(x,y)\to(1,2)} \frac{(x-1)^2(y-2)^3}{(x-1)^4+(y-2)^6}.
</md>
</p>
</statement>

<solution>
<p>
Direct substitution gives <m>\frac{0}{0}</m>, and this time nothing
cancels. To keep the algebra short, write <m>u = x - 1</m> and
<m>v = y - 2</m>. Then <m>(x,y)\to(1,2)</m> exactly when
<m>(u,v)\to(0,0)</m>, and the function becomes
<md>
f(x,y) = \frac{u^2v^3}{u^4+v^6}.
</md>
</p>
<p>
Let us first approach <m>(1,2)</m> along straight lines. Along the
vertical line <m>x = 1</m> we have <m>u = 0</m>, so <m>f(x,y) = 0</m>.
Every other line through <m>(1,2)</m> is <m>y - 2 = m(x-1)</m>, that is,
<m>v = mu</m> with <m>m \in \R</m>, and along it (with <m>u \neq 0</m>)
<md>
f(x,y) = \frac{u^2\,m^3u^3}{u^4+m^6u^6} = \frac{m^3u}{1+m^6u^2},
</md>
which tends to <m>0</m> as <m>u \to 0</m>. So <m>f(x,y) \to 0</m> as
<m>(x,y) \to (1,2)</m> along every straight line through <m>(1,2)</m>.
</p>
<p>
Straight lines are not the only paths, however. The two terms of the
denominator are equal when <m>u^4 = v^6</m>, that is, when
<m>u^2 = v^3</m>, so consider the curve <m>(x-1)^2 = (y-2)^3</m>. It can be
traced as
<md>
x = 1 + t^3, \qquad y = 2 + t^2, \qquad t \to 0,
</md>
for which <m>u = t^3</m> and <m>v = t^2</m>. Along it, for
<m>t \neq 0</m>,
<md>
f(x,y) = \frac{t^6\cdot t^6}{t^{12}+t^{12}} = \frac{1}{2},
</md>
and hence <m>f(x,y) \to \frac12</m> as <m>(x,y) \to (1,2)</m> along this
curve. The function approaches <m>0</m> along every straight line but
<m>\frac12</m> along this curve, so the limit does not exist.
</p>
</solution>
</example>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-lim-continuity">
<title>Continuity</title>

<definition xml:id="def-continuity-two-variables">
<title>Continuity at a Point and on the Domain</title>
<statement>
<p>
A function <m>f(x,y)</m> is <term>continuous at the point</term>
<m>(x_0,y_0)</m> if
</p>
<p>
<alert>1.</alert> <m>f</m> is defined at <m>(x_0,y_0)</m>,
</p>
<p>
<alert>2.</alert>
<m>\displaystyle \lim_{(x,y)\to(x_0,y_0)} f(x,y)</m> exists,
</p>
<p>
<alert>3.</alert>
<m>\displaystyle \lim_{(x,y)\to(x_0,y_0)} f(x,y) = f(x_0,y_0)</m>.
</p>
<p>
A function is <term>continuous</term> if it is continuous at every point
of its domain.
</p>
</statement>
</definition>

<example xml:id="example-cont-2xy">
<title>Continuity of <m>\frac{2xy}{x^2+y^2}</m></title>
<statement>
<p>
Show that the function
<md>
f(x,y) =
\begin{cases}
\dfrac{2xy}{x^2+y^2} \amp (x,y) \neq (0,0)\\
0 \amp (x,y) = (0,0)
\end{cases}
</md>
is continuous everywhere except at <m>(x,y) = (0,0)</m>.
</p>
</statement>

<solution>
<p>
The function is continuous at any point <m>(x,y) \neq (0,0)</m>. This is
because, at such points, it is a rational function whose denominator is
non-zero, and therefore, using the quotient rule of
<xref ref="thm-lim-properties"/>, the limit of <m>f(x,y)</m> is equal to
the value of <m>f(x,y)</m>.
</p>
<p>
To show that <m>f(x,y)</m> is discontinuous at <m>(x,y) = (0,0)</m>, we
will show that its limit does not exist at this point. Let us first
consider the limiting behavior of <m>f(x,y)</m> along <m>y = 0</m>. Since
along such a line we have <m>f(x,0) = \frac{0}{x^2+0^2} = 0</m>, we can
say that
<md xml:id="eq-cont-2xy-path1" number="yes">
f(x,y) \to 0 \quad \text{as } (x,y) \to (0,0)
\text{ along the line } y = 0.
</md>
Next we consider the limiting behavior of <m>f(x,y)</m> along
<m>y = x</m>. Since along such a line we have
<m>f(x,x) = \frac{2x^2}{x^2+x^2} = 1</m>, we can say that
<md xml:id="eq-cont-2xy-path2" number="yes">
f(x,y) \to 1 \quad \text{as } (x,y) \to (0,0)
\text{ along the line } y = x.
</md>
From <xref ref="eq-cont-2xy-path1"/> and
<xref ref="eq-cont-2xy-path2"/>, we conclude that
<m>\lim_{(x,y)\to(0,0)} f(x,y)</m> does not exist, and hence the function
is discontinuous at <m>(x,y) = (0,0)</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</example>

<example xml:id="example-cont-xy2">
<title>Continuity of <m>\frac{xy^2}{x^2+y^4}</m></title>
<statement>
<p>
Show that the function
<md>
f(x,y) =
\begin{cases}
\dfrac{xy^2}{x^2+y^4} \amp (x,y) \neq (0,0)\\
0 \amp (x,y) = (0,0)
\end{cases}
</md>
is discontinuous at <m>(x,y) = (0,0)</m>.
</p>
</statement>

<solution>
<p>
To show that <m>f(x,y)</m> is discontinuous at <m>(x,y) = (0,0)</m>, we
will show that its limit does not exist at this point.
</p>
<p>
Let us first consider the limiting behavior of <m>f(x,y)</m> along
<m>y = mx</m>, where <m>m \in \R</m>. Since along such a line we have
<m>f(x,mx) = \dfrac{m^2x^3}{x^2+m^4x^4}</m>, which simplifies to
<m>\dfrac{m^2 x}{1 + m^4 x^2}</m>, we can say that
<md xml:id="eq-cont-xy2-path1" number="yes">
f(x,y) \to 0 \quad \text{as } (x,y) \to (0,0)
\text{ along the line } y = mx.
</md>
Notice that this holds for <em>every</em> slope <m>m</m>: approaching
along any straight line gives the limit <m>0</m>.
</p>
<p>
Next, we consider the limiting behavior of <m>f(x,y)</m> along the curve
<m>x = y^2</m>, which gives
<m>f(y^2,y) = \dfrac{y^4}{y^4+y^4} = \dfrac12</m>, and we can say that
<md xml:id="eq-cont-xy2-path2" number="yes">
f(x,y) \to \frac{1}{2} \quad \text{as } (x,y) \to (0,0)
\text{ along the curve } x = y^2.
</md>
From <xref ref="eq-cont-xy2-path1"/> and
<xref ref="eq-cont-xy2-path2"/>, we conclude that
<m>\lim_{(x,y)\to(0,0)} f(x,y)</m> does not exist and hence the function
is discontinuous at <m>(x,y) = (0,0)</m>.
</p>
<p>
This example is worth pausing over: checking every straight line through
the origin is <em>not</em> enough to establish that a limit exists.
<xref ref="fig-lim-parabola-path"/> shows the family of straight paths
together with the parabola that detects the discontinuity.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-lim-parabola-path">
<caption>
Every straight line through the origin gives the limit <m>0</m>, but
along the parabola <m>x = y^2</m> the function is constantly
<m>\frac12</m>. A limit must agree along <em>all</em> paths, not merely
all straight ones.
</caption>
<image width="66%">
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-lim-parabola">
<diagram dimensions="(380,320)" margins="12">
<definition>par(t) = (t^2, t)</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-1.5,-1.85,2.6,1.85)">
<axes at="pp-axes" xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="no" arrows="1"/>
<line at="pp-l1" endpoints="((-1.25,-0.42),(1.25,0.42))" stroke="#7F7F7F"
      thickness="1.5"/>
<line at="pp-l2" endpoints="((-1.25,-1.0),(1.25,1.0))" stroke="#7F7F7F"
      thickness="1.5"/>
<line at="pp-l3" endpoints="((-1.25,0.42),(1.25,-0.42))" stroke="#7F7F7F"
      thickness="1.5"/>
<line at="pp-l4" endpoints="((-1.25,1.0),(1.25,-1.0))" stroke="#7F7F7F"
      thickness="1.5"/>
<line at="pp-l5" endpoints="((-1.25,1.6),(1.25,-1.6))" stroke="#7F7F7F"
      thickness="1.5"/>
<line at="pp-l6" endpoints="((-1.25,-1.6),(1.25,1.6))" stroke="#7F7F7F"
      thickness="1.5"/>
<parametric-curve at="pp-par" function="par" domain="(-1.35,1.35)"
                  stroke="#D55E00" thickness="3"/>
<point at="pp-O" p="(0,0)" fill="black"/>
<label at="pp-par-lab" anchor="(1.9,1.32)" alignment="e" color="#D55E00">
<m>x=y^2:\; f=\tfrac12</m>
</label>
<label at="pp-lines-lab" anchor="(-1.35,-1.55)" alignment="sw" color="#7F7F7F">
<m>y=mx:\; f\to 0</m>
</label>
<label at="pp-x" anchor="(2.45,-0.04)" alignment="se"><m>x</m></label>
<label at="pp-y" anchor="(-0.04,1.72)" alignment="nw"><m>y</m></label>
</coordinates>

<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="A coordinate plane with six grey straight lines of different slopes all passing through the origin, and one thick orange parabola opening to the right, also passing through the origin. The grey lines are labelled f tends to zero and the parabola is labelled f equals one half.">
  <annotation ref="pp-l2" text="One of the straight paths y equals m x through the origin. Along every such line the function tends to zero."/>
  <annotation ref="pp-par" text="The parabola x equals y squared. Along this curved path the function is constantly one half, which is why the limit fails to exist even though every straight line gives zero."/>
  <annotation ref="pp-O" text="The origin, where the function is defined to be zero."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
<alert>Alternative method.</alert> Alternatively, we can show that
<m>f(x,y) \to \frac12</m> as <m>(x,y) \to (0,0)</m> along the curve
<m>x = y^2</m>, and since <m>f(0,0) = 0 \neq \frac12</m>, we can conclude
that the function is not continuous at the origin by condition
<alert>3</alert> of <xref ref="def-continuity-two-variables"/>: even if
the limit existed, it would have to equal <m>f(0,0)</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</example>

<example xml:id="example-cont-find-c">
<title>Choosing <m>c</m> to Make a Function Continuous</title>
<statement>
<p>
Find <m>c</m> so that
<md>
f(x,y) =
\begin{cases}
\dfrac{(x-1)^4-(y-2)^4}{(x-1)^2+(y-2)^2} \amp (x,y) \neq (1,2)\\
c \amp (x,y) = (1,2)
\end{cases}
</md>
is continuous everywhere.
</p>
</statement>

<solution>
<p>
Factor the numerator as a difference of squares:
<md>
<mrow>(x-1)^4-(y-2)^4
\amp = \left((x-1)^2\right)^2-\left((y-2)^2\right)^2</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \left((x-1)^2-(y-2)^2\right)\left((x-1)^2+(y-2)^2\right).</mrow>
</md>
For <m>(x,y) \neq (1,2)</m>, the denominator <m>(x-1)^2+(y-2)^2</m> is
positive, so cancellation gives
<md>
f(x,y) = (x-1)^2-(y-2)^2, \qquad (x,y) \neq (1,2).
</md>
By the limit laws (<xref ref="thm-lim-properties"/>),
<md>
\lim_{(x,y)\to(1,2)} f(x,y)
= \lim_{(x,y)\to(1,2)} \left((x-1)^2-(y-2)^2\right)
= 0.
</md>
Thus continuity at <m>(1,2)</m>, which by condition <alert>3</alert> of
<xref ref="def-continuity-two-variables"/> asks that <m>f(1,2) = c</m>
equal this limit, requires
<md>
c = 0.
</md>
At every other point, <m>f</m> is already continuous because its
denominator is nonzero.
</p>
</solution>
</example>

</subsection>

</section>
